														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4825467">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Having the Axis use anomalies to fix a very real problem they had. Also I wanted to come up with a paratech article that wasn't just a weapon.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Okay but like&#8230;you understand the Axis are literal nazis right? Like they are the epitome of 'unsympathetic characters' which means you're going to have a really hard job getting someone to care about them coming up with paratech and a really hard job telling this story without glamourising the nazis because the way this is phrased implies they're gonna be the heroes here.</p>
<p>My advice is don't tell this story with the axis: find some other impetuous to have someone develop the tech (which isn't hard! there's been like four different oil crises! and even having an alternative that's less environmentally damaging could be a great like, economic opportunity or a sufficient motivation for a green organisation too!).</p>
<p>Beyond this: SCP articles are written from the perspective of the Foundation. If you want your article to be an interesting read, you're going to have to figure out where they fit in (and perhaps it's that they want the fuel source so they don't have to y'know, show up as a major purchaser of oil or handle loads of shell corporations to hide that).</p>
<p>TL;DR: if you have to put a disclaimer in your idea that you're not a nazi, it is perhaps worth considering whether the idea makes you look like a nazi unnecessarily and whether the idea glorifies the nazis.</p>
</div>
</div>
